--TEST--
Parentheses group and never change what an expression is, so a parenthesized local is still the holder an element write is written back into — `rule:types/arrays`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `nvs_syntax::ast::Expr::unparenthesized` is the one home for "parentheses
// group, and never change what an expression *is*". Two rules ask that
// question rather than what an expression evaluates to: whether a subscript
// chain's root is a place a separated array can be written back into (ADR
// 0007 § 5), and whether a read aliases storage something else already owns.
// Both are asked here at every level a parenthesis can sit at, and what is
// pinned is that they *agree* with the unparenthesized spelling — PHP writes
// through parentheses too (checked line by line against `php -r`), so
// refusing one would be a divergence bought for nothing, and reading a
// parenthesized local as a temporary released its array twice.
array<string> $a = ["0" => "x", "1" => "p"];
($a)["0"] = "y";
echo $a["0"], "\n";

// Every write spelling, through as many parentheses as the level admits.
((($a)))["0"] .= "!";
echo $a["0"], "\n";

unset(($a)["1"]);
echo Core\Arr::count($a) as string, "\n";

// A parenthesis *between* two levels of a chain leaves the root the same
// local, so the inner element is written in place rather than in a copy.
array<array<string>> $g = ["0" => ["1" => "p"]];
($g["0"])["1"] = "q";
echo $g["0"]["1"], "\n";

// The read half: `($a)` aliases the local exactly as `$a` does, so the copy
// is a second owner of one array rather than a second release of it.
array<string> $b = ($a);
$b["0"] = "z";
echo $a["0"], " ", $b["0"], "\n";
--EXPECT--
y
y!
1
q
y! z
